WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
Pick ten leaves from one white oak.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.020
Every leaf has rounded lobes.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.500
But measure them with a ruler, and you will almost surely find different lengths and

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.020
shapes. So why is no living thing exactly like another?

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.300
And are all mutations bad?

6
00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:23.243
A trait is a feature of a living thing, such as color, shape or height.

7
00:00:24.143 --> 00:00:28.143
A population is a group of the same kind of living thing in one area.

8
00:00:29.303 --> 00:00:32.123
Members of a population share the same basic features.

9
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:34.363
But they differ in many traits.

10
00:00:35.163 --> 00:00:38.623
Variation means the differences among members of a population.

11
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:41.687
Some variation comes from genes.

12
00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:44.587
Many genes carry the code for a protein.

13
00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:47.427
Some variation comes from the environment.

14
00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:49.547
Height shows both.

15
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:51.747
Many genes add a little to height.

16
00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:54.707
Food and health during childhood matter too.

17
00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:59.227
So two children with very similar genes can grow to different heights.

18
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.290
New genetic variation starts with a mutation.

19
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.050
A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence, the order of the bases.

20
00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.230
It can change the protein a gene codes for.

21
00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.030
Copying mistakes, radiation, or some chemicals can cause mutations.

22
00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.330
Mutations are random.

23
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.950
Random means they happen by chance, not because a living thing needs them.

24
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.783
Where a mutation happens matters.

25
00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:31.383
Body cells are all the cells of the body except eggs, sperm and the cells that make

26
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:32.923
them, such as skin cells.

27
00:01:33.883 --> 00:01:35.343
Sex cells are eggs and sperm.

28
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:38.803
A mutation in a skin cell stays in that person.

29
00:01:39.723 --> 00:01:42.743
A mutation in a sex cell can pass to offspring, the young.

30
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:45.273
Does a mutation help?

31
00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:48.793
That often depends on the living thing and where it lives.

32
00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:51.213
Think of a misprint in a recipe.

33
00:01:52.213 --> 00:01:53.493
Most misprints change little.

34
00:01:54.393 --> 00:01:55.393
Some ruin the dish.

35
00:01:56.013 --> 00:01:57.213
A rare one makes it better.

36
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:01.173
But a recipe has a writer, and no one chooses a mutation.

37
00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:04.613
A mutation can help, harm or make no difference.

38
00:02:05.453 --> 00:02:06.993
So not all mutations are bad.
